{"record": "end", "run_id": "20261116T081349Z_dev-02_staged_1670", "reason": "answered", "detail": "", "answer": {"answer": "No. The two analyses use different thresholds. The dSph gamma-ray limit is a one-sided 95% upper limit. It is set where 2[ln L_max − ln L(⟨σv⟩95)] = 2.71, a TS drop of 2.71 (1.355 in ln L). The AMS-02 antiproton limit is the upper endpoint of the ΔQ ≤ 3.84 region, where Q = −2 ln L. The paper describes 3.84 as the nominal two-sided 95% threshold for one parameter of interest. It says this differs from the usual one-sided 95% prescription of 2.71.\n\nThe profiled nuisance parameters also differ. The dSph analysis profiles over each dwarf's J-factor normalization. This is written J = s·J0 and profiled over log10 s in [−3, 3], with a Gaussian prior from the stellar-kinematic J uncertainty. The antiproton analysis profiles over the Galactic diffusive-halo half-height L. L has a Gaussian penalty in log10 L, centred on 4.96 kpc with σ = 0.197, and is profiled over 1–12 kpc.", "found": true, "evidence": [{"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "4.2"}, {"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "4.3"}, {"arxiv_id": "2610.26648v1", "section_id": "5"}]}, "turns": 5, "seconds": 10.316, "tool_calls": 5, "tool_result_chars": 22668, "input_tokens": 12, "output_tokens": 868, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 12424, "cache_read_input_tokens": 25198, "parser_versions": ["4"], "response_models": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "ended_at": "2026-11-16T08:14:00+00:00"}
